# 7. ADD
# Similar to COPY, but has additional features
# Prefer COPY for normal file copying
ADD website.tar.gz /app/

ADD can copy a normal file
ADD config/ /app/config/
ADD app.py /app/
Result: /app/app.py

If you write: ADD app.tar /app/
Docker can automatically extract the tar archive.

ADD can use URLs
ADD has historically supported URLs as a source, for example:
ADD https://example.com/app.tar.gz /app/

However, don't normally use ADD just to download files. For downloads, RUN curl or RUN wget gives you better control and reproducibility.
For example: RUN curl -L https://example.com/app.tar.gz -o /tmp/app.tar.gz
This downloads the file inside the image being built:
Image filesystem
└── tmp/
    └── app.tar.gz

Then if you write: COPY app.tar.gz .
Docker does not copy /tmp/app.tar.gz.
Why? Because COPY takes its source from the build context on your host, not from the previous RUN command inside the image.

If you want to move the downloaded file
Since the file is already inside the image, use another RUN:
RUN curl -L https://example.com/app.tar.gz -o /tmp/app.tar.gz && \
    cp /tmp/app.tar.gz /app/

Or simply download directly where you need it:
RUN curl -L https://example.com/app.tar.gz -o /app/app.tar.gz
