# 10. USER
# Run the application as a particular user
USER appuser
means: Run the application/process inside the container as the user appuser, instead of the default user.

Example:

FROM ubuntu
RUN useradd -m appuser
USER appuser
CMD ["bash"]

Here:
FROM ubuntu
    ↓
Default user is usually root
    ↓
USER appuser
    ↓
Now commands/processes run as appuser
    ↓
CMD runs as appuser
Why use USER?

Mainly for security.
If your application runs as root, it has very high permissions inside the container.
Instead: USER appuser
runs it with a normal user's permissions.

Important
USER does not create the user.
You need to create it first:
RUN useradd -m appuser
USER appuser

So remember:
RUN useradd → creates the user
USER appuser → switches to that user for subsequent instructions/runtime.

What happens?
During build:
RUN useradd -m appuser
        ↓
appuser is created INSIDE the image

Then:
USER appuser

means:
Container starts
      ↓
CMD runs as appuser


So when you run: docker run -it myimage
you get a shell as: appuser

not root.
Can another user start the container?
Yes.

The person on the host can run: docker run myimage
They don't need to be named appuser.
The distinction is:
HOST
└── You run: docker run myimage
             ↓
CONTAINER
└── Process runs as: appuser

Can you override the user?
Yes, if you have the required Docker permissions: docker run -it --user root myimage
Then the process runs as root instead.


What permissions does appuser have?
By default, useradd creates a normal, non-root user.

So:
root
 ├── full privileges
 │
 └── appuser
      └── normal/limited privileges

Typically, appuser cannot:
Install packages with apt
Modify system files
Change /etc files
Perform root-level operations
Access everything owned by root


-m in:  useradd -m appuser
means:  Create a home directory for the user.

Without -m: useradd appuser
the user is created, but a home directory may not be created automatically.

With -m: useradd -m appuser
you get something like:
/home/
└── appuser/

Not directly. A host/terminal user and a container user are different user accounts.
Suppose your terminal/host has:
Host
└── Siva

Then your Dockerfile:
FROM ubuntu
USER Siva

will not normally work, because the Ubuntu image doesn't know who Siva is.
The container has its own /etc/passwd and its own users.
You need to create the user inside the image


But there is one exception
You can tell Docker to run the container with a numeric UID from the host:
docker run --user 1000 myimage
This does not mean Docker copies your host user into the container. It simply tells the container:
Run the process with UID 1000.


If you mean without writing USER appuser, then the default user depends on the base image.
For your example:
FROM ubuntu
RUN useradd -m appuser
CMD ["bash"]

Since the Ubuntu base image defaults to root, the container runs as root.
FROM ubuntu
    ↓
Default user = root
    ↓
RUN useradd -m appuser
    ↓
appuser is CREATED
    ↓
But we did NOT switch to appuser
    ↓
CMD runs as root

So even though appuser exists:

Users inside container:
root       ← current/default user
appuser    ← created user, but not being used

To make appuser the runtime user: USER appuser
