Suppose your Dockerfile has:

ARG NODE_VERSION=20
FROM node:${NODE_VERSION}

Now you run:

docker build --build-arg NODE_VERSION=22 -t myapp:v2 .

During this build only, Docker uses:

NODE_VERSION = 22

So effectively:

FROM node:22

But the actual Dockerfile still contains:

ARG NODE_VERSION=20

It is not changed.

Next build

If you run:

docker build -t myapp:v3 .

without --build-arg, Docker uses the Dockerfile's default:

NODE_VERSION = 20
Think of it like this
Dockerfile:
ARG NODE_VERSION=20
          ↓
     default value


Build command:
--build-arg NODE_VERSION=22
          ↓
temporarily overrides default
          ↓
this build uses 22

So:

Dockerfile value = default
--build-arg value = override for that build only
Dockerfile itself is never modified.

Both are possible, but where you put ARG matters.

1. ARG before FROM

If you want to use the ARG inside the FROM, put it before FROM:

ARG NODE_VERSION=20


FROM node:${NODE_VERSION}

Here:

ARG NODE_VERSION=20
        ↓
FROM node:${NODE_VERSION}
        ↓
FROM node:20

This is why ARG NODE_VERSION is placed at the top in this example.

2. ARG in the middle

You can also define an ARG after FROM:

FROM ubuntu


ARG VERSION=1.0


RUN echo "Version is $VERSION"

Here ARG is available to instructions after it, such as RUN, COPY, etc.

Important rule
ARG NODE_VERSION=20
FROM node:${NODE_VERSION}

ARG before FROM → can be used in FROM.

But:
FROM node
ARG VERSION=1.0
RUN echo $VERSION

ARG after FROM → available from that point onward.

Easy memory
ARG before FROM
     ↓
Can be used by FROM


ARG after FROM
     ↓
Can be used by later instructions

So in your example:

ARG NODE_VERSION=20
FROM node:${NODE_VERSION}

it must be before FROM because you're using the variable to decide which base image to use.



But the important difference is:

ARG
 ↓
Build-time variable
 ↓
Not automatically available in running container


ENV
 ↓
Environment variable
 ↓
Available in running container


ARG
FROM ubuntu
ARG VERSION=1.0
RUN echo $VERSION

VERSION is available to instructions after the ARG line.

But:
FROM ubuntu
RUN echo $VERSION
ARG VERSION=1.0

❌ The RUN before ARG cannot use that ARG.


ENV is available during both build time and runtime. ✅
The RUN happens before ENV is defined, so $APP_VERSION is not available to that RUN.
But any instruction after ENV can use it:

FROM ubuntu
ENV APP_VERSION=1.0
RUN echo $APP_VERSION
CMD ["bash"]

Main difference
ARG → build time only
ENV → build time + runtime
