VOLUME ["/data"]
Yes, VOLUME can be placed anywhere after FROM in a Dockerfile.

For example, this is valid:

FROM ubuntu
RUN apt-get update
RUN apt-get install -y nginx
VOLUME ["/data"]
CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]

Or:

FROM ubuntu
VOLUME ["/data"]
RUN apt-get update
RUN apt-get install -y nginx
CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]

Both are valid.

But remember the important rule
VOLUME affects instructions after it.

For example:

FROM ubuntu
VOLUME ["/data"]
RUN echo "hello" > /data/file.txt

The /data location is declared as a volume before the RUN.

So the simple rule is:

FROM
 ↓
Other Dockerfile instructions
 ↓
VOLUME can be declared
 ↓
Later instructions can use that location
 ↓
CMD / ENTRYPOINT

Unlike ARG before FROM, VOLUME does not have to be at the top.

so after other docker file instruction we can give volume

Yes. ✅ You can put VOLUME after other Dockerfile instructions, as long as it comes after FROM.

For example:

FROM ubuntu
RUN apt-get update
RUN apt-get install -y nginx
ENV APP_HOME=/app
COPY . /app
VOLUME ["/data"]
CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]

This is valid.

Simple rule
FROM
 ↓
RUN
COPY
ENV
WORKDIR
...
 ↓
VOLUME
 ↓
CMD / ENTRYPOINT

VOLUME doesn't need to be at the top.
Just remember one special case:
ARG before FROM
can be required when that ARG is used in FROM.
But VOLUME can be declared later in the Dockerfile.


In Dockerfile
You cannot write: VOLUME ["mydata:/data"]   ❌
Instead, Dockerfile uses: VOLUME ["/data"]
This only declares /data as a volume mount point


If you're specifically talking about:

VOLUME ["/data"]

then it depends on how you remove the container.

Normal docker rm
docker rm mycontainer

➡️ Container is deleted.
➡️ The anonymous volume created for /data is not automatically removed.

docker rm -v
docker rm -v mycontainer
➡️ Container is deleted.
