HEALTHCHECK CMD curl -f http://localhost:8080/health || exit 1

It means Docker will periodically check whether your application is working properly.

Example:   HEALTHCHECK CMD curl -f http://localhost:8080/health || exit 1
Break it down:
HEALTHCHECK
    ↓
Tell Docker to check the container's health

CMD
    ↓
Run this command for the health check

curl -f http://localhost:8080/health
    ↓
Try to access the application's health URL

|| exit 1
    ↓
If curl fails → return failure

Example
Suppose your application is running:
Container
└── Application
    └── port 8080
         └── /health

Docker checks:
http://localhost:8080/health
If it responds successfully:  HEALTHY ✅
If it doesn't respond/fails:  UNHEALTHY ❌

You can see the result with: docker ps

You may see:
Up 2 minutes (healthy)
or:
Up 2 minutes (unhealthy)

Important

HEALTHCHECK does not restart the container by itself.
It only tells Docker:    "Is my application healthy or not?"

So:
Application working
       ↓
healthcheck succeeds
       ↓
healthy ✅

Application not responding
       ↓
healthcheck fails
       ↓
unhealthy ❌


Correct command:  in outside
docker inspect --format='{{json .State.Health}}' mycontainer
Break it down
docker inspect
      ↓
Get detailed information about the container

--format
      ↓
Show only the information we request

{{json .State.Health}}
      ↓
Show the container's health information as JSON

mycontainer
      ↓
Container name
Example output

If you have a health check:

{
  "Status": "healthy",
  "FailingStreak": 0,
  "Log": []
}

The important part is: healthy
Other possible statuses:
starting
healthy
unhealthy
One important requirement
.State.Health exists only if the container has a HEALTHCHECK configured.
For example in a Dockerfile: HEALTHCHECK CMD curl -f http://localhost:80 || exit 1
Then Docker can report:
healthy
If no health check is configured, .State.Health will not contain health information.


This command:
docker exec -it containerid pkill nginx
means go inside the running container and kill the Nginx process.

If Nginx was the main process of the container, the container may then stop, because a Docker container normally runs as long as its main process (PID 1) is running.
Why would we use this?
It's commonly used for testing.

For example, if you configured a Docker HEALTHCHECK, you can deliberately kill Nginx:
docker exec -it containerid pkill nginx
Then check: docker ps

or:

docker inspect --format='{{json .State.Health}}' containerid
This lets you see how Docker reacts when the application inside the container becomes unhealthy.



Nginx is not PID 1:
Container
├── PID 1 → application ✅
└── nginx ❌
              ↓
        Container continues

This distinction is important when testing Docker HEALTHCHECK: killing a non-PID-1 application process 
can make the health check become unhealthy while the container itself remains running.
if Nginx is only an additional component and your application does not depend on it, deleting Nginx does not affect the application.

For example:

Application Container
└── Application → PID 1 ✅

If you had:

Application Container
├── Application → PID 1 ✅
└── Nginx → additional

and you run:

pkill nginx

then:

Application → still running ✅
Nginx → stopped ❌
Container → still running ✅

So in this setup, don't worry about Nginx.
So now it is healthy or unhealthy? What is the condition?

It depends on what your HEALTHCHECK is checking.

If your health check checks Nginx

For example:

HEALTHCHECK CMD curl -f http://localhost:80 || exit 1

If you kill Nginx:

docker exec -it containerid pkill nginx

then:

Container → RUNNING ✅
Nginx     → NOT working ❌
Healthcheck → UNHEALTHY ❌

Because the health check is asking:

"Is Nginx responding on port 80?"

If your health check checks your actual application

For example, your application runs on port 8080:

HEALTHCHECK CMD curl -f http://localhost:8080/health || exit 1

Then killing Nginx:

Nginx       → NOT working ❌
Application → Working ✅
Healthcheck → HEALTHY ✅
The important rule

Healthy/unhealthy depends on what your health check tests.

